1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:11,999
The companies that navigate these crises best are the ones that prepare — building deep visibility into their supply chains and planning for the unexpected before it arrives.

2
00:00:11,999 --> 00:00:17,675
One of the most effective recovery strategies is supplier diversification.

3
00:00:17,801 --> 00:00:26,875
Rather than relying on a single source, forward-thinking companies build resilient networks across multiple regions and partners.

4
00:00:26,875 --> 00:00:40,152
Toyota learned from the 2011 earthquake in Japan to develop a more robust, multi-tier supplier strategy — dramatically reducing the impact of any single point of failure.

5
00:00:40,152 --> 00:00:55,144
The global semiconductor shortage of 2020 to 2022 accelerated massive investment in domestic chip manufacturing — as governments and corporations responded with billions in new facility funding.

6
00:00:55,144 --> 00:00:58,317
Technology is also transforming resilience.

7
00:00:58,477 --> 00:01:09,090
Real-time supply chain visibility platforms now give companies early warning of disruptions — days or even weeks before a crisis hits their inventory.

8
00:01:09,090 --> 00:01:24,639
The companies that bounced back fastest shared one common trait: they had invested in flexibility before the crisis hit — agile sourcing, redundant logistics routes, and data-driven decision making.

9
00:01:24,639 --> 00:01:27,999
Supply chains will always face disruptions.

10
00:01:28,139 --> 00:01:36,955
But with the right strategies in place, businesses can absorb the shocks, adapt faster — and emerge stronger on the other side.
